{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Study, designation, and funding: three interstate highway acts","claim":"Three federal highway acts had distinct roles: studying a proposed route network, authorizing network designation, and providing construction funding.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction","narrative":"The development of the U.S. interstate highway system involved distinct legislative steps: studying a proposed six-route toll network, authorizing designation of a much larger national network, and later providing funding for highway construction.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The title accurately summarizes the three separate acts and their functions described in the piece and source.","materiality":"Frames the central distinction among the acts."},{"excerpt":"The federal actions associated with the U.S. interstate highway system had distinct roles: studying a proposed route network, authorizing designation of a national network, and providing for highway-construction funding.","claim":"Federal actions associated with the interstate system had distinct roles in studying a proposed route network, authorizing national-network designation, and providing construction funding.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction","narrative":"The development of the U.S. interstate highway system involved distinct legislative steps: studying a proposed six-route toll network, authorizing designation of a much larger national network, and later providing funding for highway construction.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This is a faithful synthesis of the three acts' stated functions.","materiality":"Establishes the piece's organizing distinction."},{"excerpt":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the chief of the Bureau of Public Roads to study a proposed six-route toll network.","claim":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrative":"In 1938, the Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The actor, directive, year, and scope match the source.","materiality":"Identifies the first legislative step."},{"excerpt":"That study concerned the six proposed routes; it did not define all later interstates as toll roads.","claim":"The 1938 study was limited to the six proposed toll routes and did not establish that all later interstates were toll roads.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network.","narrative":"Keep the six-route toll network specific to the 1938 study; do not characterize all later interstates as toll routes.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source identifies the six-route toll network as the subject of the study, not as a description of all later interstates. The piece appropriately limits the toll-road characterization to that study.","materiality":"Prevents conflating the proposed study network with the later national system."},{"excerpt":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","claim":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","narrative":"The 1944 Act authorized designation of 40,000 miles for a national interstate network.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The mileage and authorization are accurately stated.","materiality":"Describes the scope of the designation authorization."},{"excerpt":"The 1956 Act authorized highway-construction funding and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks, and buses.","claim":"The 1956 Act authorized highway-construction funding and amended tax provisions covering motor fuel, tires, trucks, and buses.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks and buses.","narrative":"The 1956 Act authorized highway-construction funding and amended tax provisions for motor fuel, tires, trucks, and buses.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The funding purpose and listed tax subjects match the source.","materiality":"Describes the third act's funding and tax provisions."},{"excerpt":"The National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","claim":"The National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","category":"attribution","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","narrative":"The National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"This accurately attributes the characterization to the National Archives rather than presenting it as an unqualified description of completion.","materiality":"Provides an attributed description of the 1956 law's significance."},{"excerpt":"Together, the acts describe separate steps: a feasibility study, network designation, and a mechanism for financing construction—not a single act that completed the system.","claim":"The acts describe separate steps—study, network designation, and construction financing—and do not amount to a single act that completed the system.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The 1938 Federal-Aid Highway Act directed the Bureau of Public Roads chief to study a proposed six-route toll network. The 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act authorized designating 40,000 miles for a national interstate network. The 1956 Act, dated June 29, authorized funding for highway construction","narrative":"The sequence distinguishes feasibility study, network designation, and a mechanism for construction financing; the National Archives identifies the 1956 law with establishing the interstate system.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source distinguishes the study, designation authorization, and funding authorization; the piece does not mistake those actions for completed construction or a completed system.","materiality":"The distinction between authorization and completion is central to the piece."}]}

